const TBE_JOURNALS = [{"name":"TBE Solo: Quick Reference","page":"How to play solo in Foundry","content":"<h2>The Broken Empires, solo in Foundry</h2>\n<p>Everything in this pack is a macro or a roll table. Nothing overrides your game system, so it works on top of Call of Cthulhu 7e, Custom System Builder, or anything else.</p>\n\n<h3>The loop</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Frame the scene.</b> Run <b>TBE: Subverted Scene</b>, state your expectation, take what the die gives you.</li>\n<li><b>Act.</b> Roll with <b>TBE: Skill Roll</b> or <b>TBE: Opposed Roll</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Ask when you don't know.</b> <b>Ask the Weave</b>, with odds. Phrase the question so a Yes creates a complication.</li>\n<li><b>Interpret.</b> Doubles on the oracle fire a Random Event automatically. Go with your first instinct.</li>\n<li><b>Keep the list current.</b> Add NPCs, goals, and threads to <b>TBE: Empires List</b> as they matter, cross them off when they don't.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Ask the Weave chart</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"3\">\n<tr><th>Odds</th><th>Extreme Yes</th><th>Yes</th><th>No</th><th>Extreme No</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Certain</td><td>01-25</td><td>26-90</td><td>91-98</td><td>99-00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Likely</td><td>01-17</td><td>18-70</td><td>71-93</td><td>94-00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>50/50</td><td>01-10</td><td>11-50</td><td>51-90</td><td>91-00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unlikely</td><td>01-07</td><td>08-30</td><td>31-83</td><td>84-00</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Impossible</td><td>01-02</td><td>03-10</td><td>11-75</td><td>76-00</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Doubles (11, 22 ... 99, 00) generate a Random Event on top of the answer.</p>\n\n<h3>What the roll macros implement</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Roll d100 under modified skill. Success Levels = the tens die, minimum 1 on any success.</li>\n<li>Critical success: doubles under the skill, or the skill value exactly. Adds +3 SL.</li>\n<li>Critical failure: doubles over the skill.</li>\n<li>01-05 always succeeds; 99-00 always fails. Skill 0 or less still succeeds on 01-04 and crits on 05.</li>\n<li>Skill over 100 cannot critically fail and adds bonus SL equal to the tens digit of the excess, minimum 1.</li>\n<li>Opposed rolls apply the full resolution ladder: SLs, then criticals, then higher die, then higher modified skill. Degree of Success = winner's SL minus loser's SL.</li>\n<li>Fixed Numbers act as an automatic success at that many SL, and lose ties to a rolled skill.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Extended Rolls &amp; Social Encounters</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>TBE: Extended Roll</b> (Ch.2) &mdash; a task that takes more than one roll: crafting, research, tracking, anything with a target Success Level total and an interval. Start one, have each participant \"make an attempt\" during the interval, then \"resolve the interval\": the single highest successful roll counts in full, up to two more successful rolls that same interval each add +1 SL, and a critical failure anywhere in the interval halves the accumulated total (round up). Reaching the required SLs succeeds; running out an optional Limit of intervals fails.</li>\n<li><b>TBE: Social Encounter</b> (Ch.13) &mdash; the same engine applied to a scene. <i>Static</i>: a hidden Tolerance (how many attempts, success or fail, before the NPC ends the audience) and a running SL total compared to an outcome table once the scene ends. The same skill auto-fails on a third consecutive use. Exceeding Tolerance ends things immediately and knocks 1d10 off the total first. <i>Competitive</i>: two or more sides each track their own total; a successful roll adds to your own side or subtracts from a rival's. Two sides race to a Victory Condition and the winner's Degree of Success (winner minus loser) sets the outcome tier; three or more sides (a council vote, say) resolve by whoever has the most support when the GM calls it, no DoS table needed.</li>\n<li>Both tools remember their state in the world between macro runs, so a negotiation or a months-long crafting project can span more than one session. The running totals are shown openly in the dialog and the chat card, the same as everything else in this pack &mdash; if you're running a group and want the book's Tolerance-is-secret rule to actually hold, keep this dialog to yourself the way you would a GM screen.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Solo house rules worth taking</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Enhanced Defense, Solo</b> and <b>Solo Constitution</b> (d20 Wound Die) free for your PC.</li>\n<li><b>Lone Wanderer</b> free if you use the full Travel rules.</li>\n<li>+2 NPC Tolerance in static social encounters.</li>\n<li>Hire NPC allies early. Solo combat is lethal.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Tables in this pack</h3>\n<p>TBE: Random Events, Event Randomizers I and II, Subverted Expectations, Opposing Difficulty, Empires List. Edit any of them freely, the macros read whatever is in the table.</p>\n"},{"name":"TBE: Status & Peril Reference","page":"Statuses, wounds, and perils","content":"<h2>TBE Status &amp; Peril Reference</h2>\n<p>Every status this pack can put on a token, grouped the way the book presents them (Ch.10 Combat, Ch.11 Wounds, Healing &amp; Perils, Ch.14 Weave Magic). \"Auto\" means <b>TBE: Attack</b>, <b>TBE: Wounds &amp; Recovery</b>, or <b>TBE: Cast</b> applies and clears it for you as part of the roll. \"Manual\" means nothing computes it yet &mdash; mark or clear it yourself from <b>TBE: Status Effects</b> when it comes up at the table. Open <b>TBE: Status</b> any time to see everything currently active on your character in one card, and to repaint the token icons if anything ever looks stale.</p>\n\n<h3>Wounds &amp; Impairment (p.172&ndash;173)</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\">\n<tr><th>Status</th><th>How it triggers</th><th>How it clears</th><th>Source</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Impaired: Body / R Arm / L Arm / R Leg / L Leg / Head</b></td><td>Wound Die + Toughness &le; the location's total WP</td><td>Heal &rarr; Remove an impairment, or the wound closing to 0 WP</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Stunned</b></td><td>First-time Head impairment on an <i>odd</i> Wound Die: lose your next action, can still defend or move</td><td>Automatic after one round</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Arm Useless</b></td><td>First-time Arm impairment on an <i>even</i> Wound Die: drop what's held, and the arm can't hold a weapon/shield or make any roll that needs it</td><td>Heal &rarr; Remove an impairment there</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Leg Hobbled</b></td><td>First-time Leg impairment on an <i>even</i> Wound Die: still standing, but Run/Charge are impossible</td><td>Heal &rarr; Remove an impairment there</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shock</b></td><td>A second impairment anywhere, a Head impairment on an even Wound Die, or a failed Body Endurance roll. Drops the character Prone and nearly helpless. A PC may spend 3 Resolve to refuse it once.</td><td>Heal &rarr; Bring them out of Shock</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dying</b></td><td>Total lethal WP exceeds Lethality Level (&#8968;Death Threshold &divide; 3&#8969;) while in Shock. Roll Wound Die + Toughness each round; die if &le; rounds elapsed. Natural 10 stabilizes.</td><td>WP reduced to Lethality Level or below (still in Shock)</td><td>Reflected on TBE: Status; full round-by-round tracking is manual</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Infected</b></td><td>Infection check (Journey arrival, Daily Time critical-failure on Recovery, travel Infection Event, or an infectious attack)</td><td>Heal &rarr; Purge infection from a wound</td><td>Auto (checks run from Wounds &amp; Recovery / Journey Leg)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Septic</b></td><td>A Sepsis check (d20 + Toughness vs. total infected WP) fails. &minus;30 to all skills; dies in 48 hours unless treated (Heal at &minus;10, twice) or amputated (emergency Heal at &minus;20)</td><td>Successful purge (costs 1 Lethality Level permanently) or amputation</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Combat Maneuver Riders (Ch.10)</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\">\n<tr><th>Status</th><th>What it does</th><th>Clears</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Unbalanced</b></td><td>&minus;20 to the target's next roll</td><td>After their next roll (remove by hand once spent)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Locked</b></td><td>No Fighting Withdrawal for the target</td><td>End of the exchange</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Disarmed</b></td><td>Weapon or shield knocked away</td><td>When they recover the item</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Disadvantaged</b></td><td>Compel Surrender succeeded: they fight at a disadvantage</td><td>They yield, or the fight ends</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Magic (Ch.14 &mdash; Weave Reaction Table)</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\">\n<tr><th>Status</th><th>Trigger</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Restrained</b></td><td>Weave Reaction result 18 (\"Restrained for 1d4 rounds\"). TBE: Cast rolls the duration and applies it to the caster automatically.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p style=\"font-size:12px\">Fatigue-from-Reaction results (e.g. \"1 Fatigue\", \"1d4 Fatigue\") add straight to the Fatigue count below, automatically, from TBE: Cast.</p>\n\n<h3>Fatigue &amp; Travel (p.188&ndash;190)</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\">\n<tr><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th><th>Clears</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fatigued</b> (a count, not on/off)</td><td>Marks against the Resolve track, right to left, from travel and exertion. When the whole track is spent-or-Fatigued, further Fatigue instead becomes a Weary/Deprived/Sleepless wound.</td><td>Rest: Safe removes 3, Neutral 2, Precarious 1, Dangerous 0</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Weary</b></td><td>1-point lethal wound to Body from ordinary Fatigue overflow. Lethal (counts toward Death Threshold and Infection checks) but never itself becomes infected.</td><td>Only by resting &mdash; falls by the same amount of Fatigue rest removes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Deprived</b></td><td>1-point lethal wound to Body from lack of rations</td><td>A successful Rations roll, or otherwise eating</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sleepless</b></td><td>1-point lethal wound to Head from lack of sleep</td><td>8 hours of uninterrupted sleep</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p style=\"font-size:12px\">All three fatigue-wounds: never require a Wound Die roll to take or increase, never stack more than one instance, and can't be treated with Heal or Recovery rolls &mdash; only rest/rations/sleep as listed.</p>\n\n<h3>Other Perils (Ch.11, p.187&ndash;191)</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\">\n<tr><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Blinded</b></td><td>&minus;50 to all sight-based skills; ranged attacks impossible</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Deafened</b></td><td>&minus;50 to all hearing/speech-based skills, including casting Weave spells or divine miracles</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Frostbite</b></td><td>Cold-based wound from extreme exposure (1d3 to a random location per hour, no armour protection)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Burning</b></td><td>Ongoing Burn wound from fire</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Poisoned</b></td><td>Non-wounding poison or disease effect (paralysis, nausea, etc). A Challenging (&minus;10) Heal roll can halve its remaining duration, once.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Feared</b></td><td>Failed an opposed Willpower roll vs. a Fear Rating: Fight (all rolls at &minus;20, no maneuvers), Flee (move away, no other actions), or Fall (drop Prone, defend only)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p style=\"font-size:12px\">None of these are rolled automatically yet &mdash; mark them from TBE: Status Effects when they come up, and clear them the same way once the scene resolves.</p>\n\n<h3>Position &amp; Consciousness</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\">\n<tr><th>Status</th><th>How it triggers</th><th>How it clears</th><th>Source</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Prone</b></td><td>First-time Leg impairment on an <i>odd</i> Wound Die, dropping in Shock, or a maneuver rider (Trip, Shield Bash)</td><td>Standing back up (a move action), or Heal &rarr; Bring them out of Shock</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Unconscious</b></td><td>First-time Head impairment on an <i>even</i> Wound Die, or dropping in Shock from a hit to the Head</td><td>Heal &rarr; Bring them out of Shock</td><td>Auto</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dead</b></td><td>The Death Threshold reaches 0</td><td>GM's call &mdash; toggle it off the token</td><td>Auto (TBE: Attack, TBE: Quick Combat)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p style=\"font-size:12px\">On the native the-broken-empires system, Prone and Unconscious are real TBE statuses (the token icon is the record, same as every other row on this page) and Dead reuses Foundry's own built-in \"dead\" status effect &mdash; none of the three live in a separate condition box anymore.</p>\n"},{"name":"TBE: Weave Magic Reference","page":"Shaping costs, effects, Weave Reactions, Fraying","content":"<h2>TBE: Weave Magic Reference</h2>\n<p>Every number here is generated from the rulebook extract the macros themselves use, so this page and <b>TBE: Cast</b> can never disagree. Cast prices a spell for you and rolls the Weave Reaction; this page is for reading ahead, and for the GM adjudicating an Effect the list does not cover.</p>\n<h3>Shaping a spell</h3>\n<p>Before it can be cast, a spell must be shaped by the following elements, each one contributing to its Total Cost (TC).</p>\n<h4>Magnitude</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Discreet</td><td>free</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subtle</td><td>+2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Offensive</td><td>+5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vulgar</td><td>+10 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Target</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Self</td><td>free</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 individual</td><td>+2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 zone</td><td>+3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 zones</td><td>+5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 zones</td><td>+7 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Structure</td><td>+9 TC, Ritual only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bounded Area</td><td>+10 TC, Ritual only, +1d6 Fraying</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Range</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Self</td><td>free</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Touch/Engaged</td><td>+1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Close/Short</td><td>+2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Medium/Long</td><td>+3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very Long</td><td>+4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sight</td><td>+5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arcane Tether</td><td>+6 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Duration</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Instant</td><td>free</td></tr>\n<tr><td>One Round (6 seconds)</td><td>+1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Concentration</td><td>+2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Minute (10 rounds)</td><td>+3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Hour</td><td>+4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sun (to the next Sunup/Sundown)</td><td>+5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week</td><td>+6 TC, Ritual only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fortnight</td><td>+7 TC, Ritual only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Month</td><td>+8 TC, Ritual only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Year</td><td>+9 TC, Ritual only, +1d4 Fraying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Permanent</td><td>+10 TC, Ritual only, +2d6 Fraying</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Per-target and per-instance costs</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Surcharge</th><th>Cost</th></tr><tr><td>Additional individual targets</td><td>+2 TC each</td></tr><tr><td>Choose the struck location (per target, chosen after the roll)</td><td>+2 TC each</td></tr><tr><td>Individuals in a targeted zone exempted from the Effect</td><td>+2 TC each</td></tr><tr><td>Ignore shield AP (per target, after the roll)</td><td>+2 TC each</td></tr><tr><td>Ignore up to 3 armor AP in the struck location (per target, after the roll)</td><td>+1 TC each</td></tr><tr><td>Triggered Effect (delay the Effect until a described condition)</td><td>+1 TC each, up to +9</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Worn armor.</b> Initiative penalty of worn armor acts as a positive modifier to every spell&#x27;s TC.</p>\n<h3>Control</h3>\n<p>Add together the Bind roll&#x27;s ones die, the relevant Strand value, and any Thread result to find the caster&#x27;s Mastery. A &ldquo;0&rdquo; on the ones die counts as 10. The difference between the Mastery and the TC is called the Weave Reaction Modifier. Each additional Resolve spent reduces the modifier by 1. Reducing the modifier to zero cancels the Weave Reaction.</p>\n<p><b>Critical failure.</b> The spell is not cast. Spend 1 Resolve and immediately roll on the Weave Reaction Table, adding the spell&#x27;s Magnitude cost to the result. Fades also gain 1 Fraying.</p>\n<h3>Spell Effect costs</h3>\n<h4>Add Actions</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Duration must be One Round. Extra actions occur at the end of the round.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>+1 Action</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+2 Actions (+1 Fraying)</td><td>10 TC, +1 Fraying</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Armor Points</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Protection equal to worn armor; can also nullify AP, or give a weapon Piercing.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>+1 AP</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+2 AP</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+3 AP</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+4 AP</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+5 AP</td><td>8 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+6 AP</td><td>10 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Attack: External damage</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Target keeps armor and shield AP; usually resisted by Dodge. Duration must be Instant.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Damage equal to your Strand value</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Each extra point of damage above your Strand value</td><td>1 TC each</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Attack: Internal damage</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Ignores all armor and shield AP; resisted by Willpower or Endurance. Cannot exceed your Strand value.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Damage equal to your Strand value</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Attributes</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Toughness, Death Threshold, Lethality Level, Initiative, Max ENC. Resolve, Max Resolve and Fatigue can never be affected.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 1</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 2</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 3</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 4</td><td>8 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 5</td><td>10 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Initiative or Max ENC, +/- 1 per TC</td><td>1 TC each</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Enhance Weapon</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Make magical (no damage bonus)</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+1 damage</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+2 damage</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Suppress a creature&#x27;s immunity to non-magical weapons</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+3 damage</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+4 damage</td><td>8 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+5 damage</td><td>10 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Heal Damage</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Conjure Body, Duration Instant. You do not pay extra to Choose Location.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Per 1 Wound Point healed</td><td>1 TC each</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Remove infection from a specific wound</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nullify a poison or remove a disease</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Mental or Emotional</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Almost always resisted by Willpower; Thought-based on humanoids.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Detect Emotional State</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Base emotion (anger, jealousy, desire, despair, disgust, anxiety): +/-10 on situational skills</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fear (Fear Rating = the Bind roll&#x27;s SLs)</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Charm (+30 to the caster&#x27;s Social rolls except Intimidate)</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sleep (opposed Willpower; target falls Prone and cannot act)</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Higher emotion (love, compassion, loyalty, joy, gratitude, guilt): +/-20</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bond an animal (Control Beast) as a loyal servant for the Duration</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Confuse (d10 each action: 1-4 no action, 5-7 helps a foe, 8-10 attacks an ally)</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory (erase, alter, implant, force reveal, or read surface thoughts)</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dominate (act against their own interests; max 1 minute unless a ritual)</td><td>8 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Senses &amp; Illusions</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Illusions always use the Thought Strand with the Conjure Bind, and cost by the senses they engage.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 of Taste, Touch/Feel, or Scent</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sound (speech included)</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Any 3 of Taste, Touch/Feel, Scent, Sound</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sight</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Any 3 including Sight</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>All senses</td><td>8 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Size &amp; Transformation</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Each size step: +/-1 Toughness, +/-2 base damage, +/-5 Death Threshold, -/+1 Initiative.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 1 Size category</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 2 Size categories</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 3 Size categories</td><td>7 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 4 Size categories</td><td>10 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 5 Size categories (Ritual only)</td><td>15 TC, Ritual only</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Speed or Movement</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>+1 zone of movement</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+2 zones of movement</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+3 zones of movement</td><td>7 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+4 zones of movement</td><td>10 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Combat Maneuver (Unbalance, Drive Back, Disarm, Trip); Duration must be Instant</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Immobilized (cannot move, can still act)</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Restrained (cannot move or act, can defend)</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Puppet (control their movement; cannot make them harm themselves)</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vulgar movement (levitation, flight, passing through matter), on top of any other Effect</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Paralyzed (cannot move, act or defend)</td><td>8 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Conjure Substance (by trade value)</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Conjured substance vanishes when the Duration expires. Beings cannot be Conjured.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 5 sp</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 50 sp</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 100 sp</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 200 sp</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 500 sp</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 1,000 sp</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 2,000 sp</td><td>7 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 5,000 sp</td><td>8 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 10,000 sp</td><td>9 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>up to 20,000 sp</td><td>10 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Conjure Substance (by size or volume)</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>a pocketful</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>sword-sized</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>a backpack&#x27;s worth</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>a Man-sized amount</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>a Wagon-load full</td><td>7 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>to fill an Inn&#x27;s common room (Ritual only)</td><td>10 TC, Ritual only</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Summoning</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Requires a summoning circle. The Magnitude of any summoning spell is always Vulgar.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Summon an otherworldly being or departed spirit</td><td>6 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Skill Modifier</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">One skill per instance; buy the Effect again for a second skill.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 10</td><td>1 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 20</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 30</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 40</td><td>7 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>+/- 50</td><td>10 TC</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Zone Condition</h4>\n<p style=\"font-size:90%\">Targets the zone itself. Add +2 TC per individual excluded from the hazard.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Favored Zone (Favor equal to half the Strand value, rounded up)</td><td>2 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hazard: Confined, Obscured or Rough</td><td>3 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Barrier (cover against ranged attacks, may block line of sight)</td><td>4 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hazard: Blocked, or Other</td><td>5 TC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Damaging hazard, external (1 TC per damage, up to 3x your Strand)</td><td>1 TC each</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Damaging hazard, internal (1 TC per damage, up to 2x your Strand)</td><td>1 TC each</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Anything else</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Effect</th><th>Cost</th></tr>\n<tr><td>GM-assigned Effect cost (1-10 TC)</td><td>1 TC each</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h3>The Weave Reaction Table</h3>\n<p>An uncontrolled spell must roll d20 + the Weave Reaction Modifier and consult the Weave Reaction Table. The three highest results only apply if the casting qualifies; anything else lands on Catastrophic Fray. <b>TBE: Cast</b> rolls this for you and applies the Fraying, Fatigue, Supply and status results directly.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>d20 + modifier</th><th>Weave Reaction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>&le;1&ndash;1</td><td>2 pt Thread created</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Unexpected Detail</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>1 Fatigue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Cut Off for next round</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>1-point wound</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Echo: 1 unintended target</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>d6 Thread Die created</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>1d4+1 steps of Supply consumed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>9</td><td>Cut Off for 1d6 rounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10</td><td>Immobilized for 1d4 rounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11</td><td>Hallucinations</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12</td><td>1d4 Fatigue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>13</td><td>Additional spells cost +2d6 TC until sunrise/set</td></tr>\n<tr><td>14</td><td>Weave Scar: -20 to Bind skill until sunrise/set</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>Hazard created in zone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16</td><td>Echo: 1d4+1 targets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>17</td><td>1d4+1 point wound</td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>Restrained for 1d4 rounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>19</td><td>Cut Off until sunrise/set</td></tr>\n<tr><td>20</td><td>1d3 Fraying</td></tr>\n<tr><td>21</td><td>Marked: Minor deformity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>22</td><td>Reality Snag</td></tr>\n<tr><td>23</td><td>Cut Off for 1d4 days</td></tr>\n<tr><td>24</td><td>Weave Scar: -1d6+2 to Bind skill, permanently</td></tr>\n<tr><td>25</td><td>Marked: Major deformity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>26+</td><td>Catastrophic Fray</td></tr>\n<tr><td>30+ (Vulgar only)</td><td>Void Incursion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>31&ndash;35 (Ritual only)</td><td>Fraygeist</td></tr>\n<tr><td>36&ndash;40 (Ritual only)</td><td>Pattern Collapse</td></tr>\n<tr><td>41+ (Ritual only)</td><td>The Grey Wailing</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>What each result means</h4><dl>\n<dt><b>Thread created</b></dt><dd>A Thread is spontaneously created and appears in the area. It is attuned to the higher value of the Bind or Strand used in the casting roll.</dd>\n<dt><b>Unexpected Detail</b></dt><dd>Some strange minor cosmetic adjustment of the spell&#x27;s effects, that does not negate the intent of the spell.</dd>\n<dt><b>Supply dice consumed</b></dt><dd>One or more of the caster&#x27;s Supply dice are consumed by the Weave and reduced by 1 die type, distributed amongst them. Suffer 1 Fatigue for each step of die type unable to be reduced.</dd>\n<dt><b>Cut Off</b></dt><dd>The Spellweaver is cut off from the Tapestry and can&#x27;t cast another spell for the listed Duration. Ongoing spells continue; Concentration is allowed.</dd>\n<dt><b>Hallucinations</b></dt><dd>Strange visions distract the caster, preventing any action until they use an action to make a successful Willpower roll against 3 or the spell&#x27;s Bind roll, whichever is higher.</dd>\n<dt><b>Fatigued</b></dt><dd>The spell tires the caster. If the new Fatigue cannot be marked, a Weary wound is caused.</dd>\n<dt><b>Echo</b></dt><dd>The spell affects an additional unintended target contrary to the caster&#x27;s intentions and beyond his control, or its effects occur elsewhere within Range. With no other suitable target, an Echo affects the caster himself if the effect is to his detriment.</dd>\n<dt><b>Wound</b></dt><dd>The caster suffers a separate Wound to a random location. Armor Points do not protect.</dd>\n<dt><b>Immobilized/Restrained</b></dt><dd>The energies lock the caster in place, able only to defend. The caster may use their action each round to make a new opposed Willpower roll against 3 or the spell&#x27;s Bind roll, whichever is higher, to break free.</dd>\n<dt><b>Hazard created in zone</b></dt><dd>Details reflect the Bind and Strand. Hazards affect anyone in the zone including the caster and last an hour. Roll 1d6: 1-2 Obscured, 3-4 Rough, 5 Blocked, 6 Damaging (Strand value in damage).</dd>\n<dt><b>Marked</b></dt><dd>The caster is permanently marked by the Weave with a minor or major deformity to a random detailed hit location. Major deformities can affect appropriate skills by -20.</dd>\n<dt><b>Weave Scar</b></dt><dd>The Tapestry reweaves your own Pattern within it: reduce the Bind skill value used in the casting by the listed amount.</dd>\n<dt><b>Fraying</b></dt><dd>Add Fraying points as indicated.</dd>\n<dt><b>Reality Snag</b></dt><dd>Until the next sunrise or sunset the caster&#x27;s spells suffer +5 to future Weave Reaction rolls (still mitigable), and each further spell attempt buckles reality: 1d6, 1-3 spatial distortion (-20 to ranged attacks into or out of the caster&#x27;s zone for a minute), 4-5 backlash strain (1 Fatigue), 6 severe backlash (1d4 Fatigue and Willpower vs 3 or Cut Off for 1d6 rounds).</dd>\n<dt><b>Catastrophic Fray</b></dt><dd>Gain +1 Fraying per point over 25, and a spontaneous magical anomaly manifests (GM&#x27;s choice: storm, rift, bleeding light, mass hallucination). It should be local and survivable, reflect the Bind and Strand, and always leave a permanent scar.</dd>\n<dt><b>Void Incursion</b></dt><dd>Vulgar spells only, otherwise treat as Catastrophic Fray. A breach tears open and a Void Demon emerges in or near the caster&#x27;s zone, immediately trying to possess the closest living humanoid (opposed Willpower against its 75). A Spellweaver may attempt to seal the breach once per incursion with an immediate Bind: Destroy roll opposed by Willpower 75; on a success the demon is dragged back and the caster suffers 1d6 Fraying. A ritual version summons a Greater-tier demon.</dd>\n<dt><b>Fraygeist</b></dt><dd>Ritual only. An exact duplicate of the caster manifests near the casting site within 1d6 days, believing itself the true original. It retains the caster&#x27;s knowledge, power and skills, possesses duplicates of all their items, weapons and Threads, and will hunt down and destroy the original.</dd>\n<dt><b>Pattern Collapse</b></dt><dd>Ritual only. Lose 3d10 points each from 3 random skills. All owned Threads unravel and are consumed. Each participant gains +1d3 Fraying. Any future spell using the same Bind and Strand combination suffers +2 to Weave Reaction rolls.</dd>\n<dt><b>The Grey Wailing</b></dt><dd>Ritual only. The ritual&#x27;s zone and 1d6 surrounding zones are permanently transformed into a corrupted wasteland that expands by 1d6 feet each dawn. Only a Destroy Spheres ritual of TC 40+ performed at the heart of the corruption can halt its spread.</dd>\n</dl>\n<h3>Fraying</h3>\n<p><b>Roll 1d100. If the result is less than or equal to (Total Fraying - Max Resolve) × 2, the Spellweaver is forcibly purged from the Tapestry of reality.</b> There is no way of reducing Fraying points,</p>\n<p>Those with a Faded Pattern accumulate Fraying at an even faster rate: every time they roll a critical failure on a casting roll, they accumulate 1 Fraying point</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Threshold</th><th>Symptoms</th></tr><tr><td>10 Fraying points (regardless of Max Resolve)</td><td>Subtle, deniable disturbances that might be passed off as fatigue or coincidence.<ul><li>Your shadow lags behind or tilts slightly wrong.</li><li>Strangers forget your name moments after hearing it.</li><li>Your reflections ripple or appear subtly distorted.</li><li>Your footprints fade away almost as soon as they&#x27;re made.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Fraying &gt;= Max Resolve - 5</td><td>The wrongness can no longer be ignored by those nearby.<ul><li>Animals shy away, bark, or bolt from your presence.</li><li>Written words about you blur or fade from pages.</li><li>Colors look washed out when you enter a space.</li><li>Companions swear you weren&#x27;t there until you spoke.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Fraying &gt;= Max Resolve</td><td>Reality struggles to hold you in place; the unraveling is undeniable.<ul><li>Doubles of you are glimpsed in the corner of the eye.</li><li>Dreams depict you as faceless or as a stranger.</li><li>Patterns misalign: bricks, tiles, rugs subtly warp around you.</li><li>Objects near you refuse to line up</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td>Fraying &gt;= Max Resolve + 5</td><td>The Weave is actively tugging your thread loose.<ul><li>Your eyes look hollow or distant.</li><li>Whole conversations with you vanish from memory after they end.</li><li>Places you linger warp: candles drip upward, mirrors crack, walls bend, plants wither.</li><li>You feel a constant physical snag in your chest, like a thread being pulled away.</li></ul></td></tr></table>\n<p>Symptoms begin when the total first reaches a threshold and <b>remain permanently</b>, even if Max Resolve later increases.</p>\n<p><b>Frayed.</b> Once a Spellweaver accumulates 10+ Fraying points and begins to show the first symptoms, they may choose to take the “Frayed” Personality Trait. It acts in every way as a regular Personality Trait, but assumes the player is roleplaying the narrative effects above. Once chosen, this Trait may not be removed.</p>\n<p><b>The Final Act.</b> When a Spellweaver is about to be purged from the Tapestry, they may immediately attempt one last working of magic before they unravel. This act of magic is always successful: even if the Spellweaver is bound, silenced, or unconscious, the Weave itself forces the act through them.</p>\n<h3>Strands</h3>\n<p>To increase a Strand, spend XP equal to the next highest value. Improvements must be sequential. for each point a Strand is raised above 10, the Spellweaver gains 1 Fraying.</p>\n<p>a Fade&#x27;s Strands can never be developed past 7. Fades are not limited by Thin Strands. they may spend 5 XP to acquire the Strand Secret Talent, granting the Strand a starting value of 1. If the Strand is a Thin Strand, the cost is instead 10 XP.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Strand</th><th>What it governs</th></tr><tr><td><b>Air</b></td><td>Sound, electricity, cold, and light that does not produce heat. Includes invisibility.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Beast</b></td><td>Properties of all creatures, animals, and monsters native to the Broken Empires, and their products (hides, scales, teeth, etc.). Extra-planar creatures like demons fall under Spheres .</td></tr><tr><td><b>Body</b></td><td>Life, healing, properties of the humanoid physical form. Wounds healed by magic remain healed after the Duration expires.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Earth</b></td><td>Terrestrial materials, minerals, soil, metal, rock, the bones of the earth. Includes gravity.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fire</b></td><td>Light that produces heat, that which burns. Fire created by magic is normal fire, and burns until it runs out of fuel, the Duration ends, it is put out, or the spell is countered.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Plant</b></td><td>All living plants and materials derived from them.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spheres</b></td><td>The planes of existence, summoning and binding otherworldly creatures, divining through the constellations, magical transport, the minutiae and esoterica of magic itself.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spirit</b></td><td>Ghosts and the undead, but also the immortal soul, and spiritual conviction and resistance.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Thought</b></td><td>Minds, dreams, imagination, illusion, knowledge, reason, emotions. When Thought magic’s Duration expires, or if successfully resisted, the target may become aware of the magical manipulation.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Water</b></td><td>Includes precipitation, snow, ice, oceans, rivers, and non-biological liquid. 275 A Spell’s Intention There are many possible approaches to achieving a result through magic. A player should be very specific about their intent behind the magical effect, and how they imagine that spell unfolding. As a caster, I must think: “What’s my intent? And which Bind and Strand combination will I use to achiev</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Bind</th><th>What it does</th></tr><tr><td><b>Change</b></td><td>To alter, to change the basic properties of. An individual—either living being or object—may be made of component parts, but its Essential Pattern is its truest use or most honest expression. A sword has a metal blade ( Earth ), wooden handle ( Plant ), and leather wrappings ( Beast ), but in the end, its true function is about the blade—so its Essential Pattern is Earth . Therefore, casting a spe</td></tr><tr><td><b>Conjure</b></td><td>To bring forth into being, to create matter from nothing. Individual beings, living or otherwise, cannot be Conjured into existence, but such beings may be healed by this Bind; extra-planar beings may be Summoned. Conjured items last only as long as the spell’s Duration, but their effects may last beyond that.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Control</b></td><td>To manipulate or protect, to take active control. Once a thing is under your control, it remains so until the Duration ends (or until the circumstances change to grant it a new opposed roll). Generally, it takes an action to actively control something or to issue a specific command.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Destroy</b></td><td>To cause harm, to bring an end to. Things destroyed by magic stay destroyed after the spell’s Duration ends; hence, most Destroy spells that do direct or permanent damage have a Duration of Instant, but also have a higher Magnitude to reflect the spell’s destabilizing nature.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Witness</b></td><td>To perceive, to know, to divine, to detect, to scry. Witness spells are often based on what Senses they employ.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Convocations</h3>\n<p>Optional templates for building a Spellweaver at character creation (Ch.7 p.106). The <b>TBE: Character Wizard</b> rolls or applies any of these and fills the picks in for you.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Convocation</th><th>Binds</th><th>Strands</th><th>Thin Strands</th></tr><tr><td><b>Aeromancer</b></td><td>Conjure, Control</td><td>Air, Earth, Spirit, Thought</td><td>Beast, Spheres</td></tr><tr><td><b>Battlemage</b></td><td>Conjure, Destroy</td><td>Beast, Body, Earth, Fire</td><td>Spirit, Thought</td></tr><tr><td><b>Druid</b></td><td>Change, Control</td><td>Beast, Earth, Plant, Water</td><td>Spheres, Spirit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Earthbinder</b></td><td>Change, Control</td><td>Beast, Air, Earth, Plant</td><td>Spirit, Thought</td></tr><tr><td><b>Elementalist</b></td><td>Conjure, Control</td><td>Air, Earth, Fire, Water</td><td>Body, Thought</td></tr><tr><td><b>Firemancer</b></td><td>Conjure, Control</td><td>Air, Fire, Plant, Thought</td><td>Water, Body</td></tr><tr><td><b>Healer</b></td><td>Conjure, Witness</td><td>Beast, Body, Thought, Water</td><td>Spheres, Spirit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Inquisitor of the Order of the Purifying Flame</b></td><td>Destroy, Witness</td><td>Body, Fire, Spheres, Spirit</td><td>Earth, Water</td></tr><tr><td><b>Seer</b></td><td>Control, Witness</td><td>Body, Air, Spheres, Thought</td><td>Beast, Earth</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sorcerer</b></td><td>Control, Destroy</td><td>Body, Fire, Spheres, Thought</td><td>Beast, Spirit</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spiritbinder</b></td><td>Change, Control</td><td>Body, Spheres, Spirit, Thought</td><td>Plant, Fire</td></tr><tr><td><b>Wavemaster</b></td><td>Change, Control</td><td>Air, Beast, Body, Water</td><td>Fire, Spirit</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Threads</h3>\n<p>Threads are used only after a successful Bind roll (never on a failure). You may use up to one Bind Thread and one Strand Thread in a single casting. Additional Threads of the same type never stack. When using the Thread Die, roll it after a successful Bind roll, and gain whatever amount you roll as Mastery. If you roll a 1 or a 2, take that number as bonus Mastery, but reduce the Thread Die to the next lower die type. If a d6 Thread rolls a 1 or a 2, it is fully expended and crumbles to dust.</p>\n<h3>Rituals</h3>\n<p><b>TBE: Ritual</b> runs this whole procedure &mdash; casting time, the concentration rolls, every source of Mastery, the results table and the Weave Reaction. This is what it is doing.</p>\n<ul><li>The Range of any ritual must be Arcane Tether.</li><li>A ritual requires one hour of casting time per TC. If the caster wants to cut the spell&#x27;s casting time in half (hours equal to half the final TC, rounded up), any Weave Reaction roll will be made at +10.</li><li>The principal caster and all assistants take 1 Fatigue for every eight hours (or portion thereof) of casting time, and the principal caster must make a standard Willpower roll every eight hours (or portion thereof) to maintain focus.</li><li>The caster cannot spend Resolve on the Ritual casting roll &mdash; though rituals still require each participant to have at least one available Resolve.</li><li>All ritual castings automatically suffer +5 to any Weave Reaction roll, and Critically failing a ritual causes a Fraying point in addition to other costs.</li></ul>\n<h4>Buying Mastery for a ritual</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Mastery</th></tr><tr><td>Components</td><td>1&amp;ndash;8, GM&#x27;s discretion; otherwise components worth 100 sp &amp;times; the TC</td></tr><tr><td>Assistance</td><td>half each helper&#x27;s Strand value, rounded up; at most half the caster&#x27;s own Strand value in helpers</td></tr><tr><td>Grimoires</td><td>a d4&amp;ndash;d12 Thread Die per applicable Bind or Strand, once per ritual; a maximum roll consumes the book</td></tr><tr><td>Blood Magic</td><td>1 per lethal Wound Point, up to 20, +20 to the casting roll if the victim dies just before completion</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Blood Magic marks.</b> For each Wound Point inflicted upon the sacrificial victim, there is a 5% chance the caster and all assistants become marked (100% if the victim is killed). Roll d10:</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>d10</th><th>Mark</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A patch of skin permanently blackened, as if charred</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Veins that show through the skin in dark crimson or black</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Eyes that glint faintly red in firelight</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>A faint sigil burned into the palm or forehead</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Skin that appears stained with blood in streaks or blotches</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Fingernails turned permanently black</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>A brandlike scar in the shape of twisting lines or runes</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>The mouth bleeds slightly when speaking the truth</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>An aura of faint cold</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Teeth or tongue faintly discolored as if with dried blood</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Ritual Casting Results</h4>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Roll</th><th>Outcome</th></tr><tr><td><b>Success</b></td><td>The ritual is cast as intended. All participants take 1 Fatigue. The Bind roll&#x27;s SLs sets the ritual spell&#x27;s resistance.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Success, but Mastery is less than the spell&#x27;s TC</b></td><td>The ritual is cast, but not fully controlled: roll on the Weave Reaction Table with a modifier equal to the difference between the Mastery and the TC, plus any accumulated modifiers from failed concentration rolls, and +5 for the ritual itself. All participants take 3 Fatigue</td></tr><tr><td><b>Critical Success</b></td><td>The ritual is cast as intended and there is no Weave Reaction roll.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Failure</b></td><td>The ritual fails. All participants take 3 Fatigue. Components can be reused.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Critical Failure</b></td><td>The ritual fails. All participants take 6 Fatigue and 1 Fraying. All components are used up.</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Magic Circles</h4>\n<p>Inscribing a magic circle costs 1,000 sp in materials, and takes a full 8 hours There can only be one magic circle inscribed in a zone. Every 2 SLs on the Arcana roll will allow the circle to reduce the cost of any spell or ritual cast entirely within its bounds by 1 SL. Every additional 500 sp worth of materials consumed while inscribing the circle adds +10 to the Arcana roll, up to +30 for 1500 sp. Resolve may not be used on the Arcana roll. Fades cannot use magic circles.</p>\n<h4>Pacts &mdash; rituals for non-casters</h4>\n<p>Neither petitioner nor Patron has to roll for the pact ritual. Prices each have a d10 Timer Die associated with them, with roll intervals ranging from one day to one year. At the completion of the ritual, the petitioner sets the Price total at 1 and must start tracking intervals. If the result is the current Price total or less, the Price is immediately paid. If not, the petitioner adds 1 to the Price total.</p>\n<p><b>Example Prices.</b></p><ul><li>A piece of the petitioner&#x27;s lifespan, or reduction of Toughness/Death Threshold/location Lethality Level.</li><li>Loss of a cherished memory.</li><li>The petitioner or someone they care for suffers a wound or infection.</li><li>The petitioner&#x27;s voice becomes increasingly muffled or distant, imposing Social skill penalties.</li><li>Reduction of a petitioner&#x27;s skill value by 5d6.</li><li>A requirement to revisit a specific location every week/month/year and perform a costly sacrifice.</li><li>Loss of Status due to moral or spiritual corruption.</li><li>Someone the petitioner loves spurns and abandons him.</li><li>Gradual loss of distinction between dreams and reality.</li><li>A new, horrific scar appears in a prominent place on the petitioner&#x27;s body.</li><li>A relentless hunger that no food or drink can satisfy.</li><li>The petitioner&#x27;s reflection disappears, leaving her unable to see herself ever again.</li><li>At inopportune moments, the petitioner speaks the words and thoughts of the patron instead of his own.</li><li>One of the petitioner&#x27;s loved ones becomes possessed by an agent of the patron, causing them to commit acts favorable to the patron.</li><li>One of the petitioner&#x27;s arm locations slowly starts to turn into a translucent tentacle, scaled claw, or other such mutation.</li><li>The petitioner feels the suffering of others in her proximity as if it were her own.</li><li>The petitioner feels invisible eyes watching him constantly, trapping him in a perpetual state of paranoia.</li></ul>\n<h3>Summoning</h3>\n<p>The Magnitude of any summoning spell is always Vulgar (+10 TC). a unique summoning circle is drawn on the ground by the caster (a process that takes an hour and requires 1,000 sp worth of materials; a circle can only be used for one successful casting). The Conjure spell is then cast with a Duration of Instant, a Range of Arcane Tether, a Target of Individual, and a Summoning Effect (6 TC). Native beings of the Broken Empires cannot be summoned this way, except for the spirits of the dead.</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"4\"><tr><th>Circle materials</th><th>Arcana roll</th></tr><tr><td>1,000 sp</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr><tr><td>2,000 sp</td><td>+10</td></tr><tr><td>5,000 sp</td><td>+20</td></tr></table>\n<p>The being to be summoned gets an opposed Willpower roll. Using a creature&#x27;s True Name, if it has one, imposes a -20 penalty on its Willpower roll. If the caster wins, the creature is bound within the circle for a number of days equal to the caster&#x27;s Arcana Score. A summoning circle remains stable for a number of days equal to the caster&#x27;s Arcana Score. This requires one hour of uninterrupted work and a new Arcana roll.</p>\n<p>If the summoned creature is able to leave the circle without first being controlled, it will either flee by the most direct route or attempt to harm the summoner.</p>\n<h3>True Names</h3>\n<p>True Names are the ultimate connection to the Weave, and can be used as an Arcane Tether. Ordinary mortals do not have True Names, and thus cannot be magically targeted through them.</p>\n<ul><li>it must be spoken aloud in the language of that name, requiring a successful Language roll, or the spell using it automatically fails. On a critically failed Language roll, the caster also suffers an immediate Weave Reaction (roll 1d20 +10).</li><li>each time you invoke a True Name in a spell, roll 1d10; on a 1, gain 1 Fraying.</li><li>As a True Name is not a physical Arcane Tether, it is not consumed with the casting, and knowing another&#x27;s True Name does not count towards the caster&#x27;s Arcane Tether limit.</li><li>Speaking a True Name always alerts the target that it was invoked.</li><li>a True Name imposes a -20 penalty on the target&#x27;s Willpower roll to resist the summoning.</li></ul>\n"},{"name":"TBE: Rules Audit","page":"Every macro formula, quoted and page-cited","content":"<h2>TBE: Rules Audit</h2>\n<p>Every row below is generated from the rulebook extract the macros themselves compute from, not written by hand, so this page and the macros it describes can never quietly disagree. For each entry: what the macro computes, in plain language; the book chapter and page it comes from; and the exact sentence, quoted verbatim, that the macro is implementing. Page numbers are pulled from the book's own printed page markers in the source text, not typed in by hand, so a wrong page number here would mean the lookup itself is broken, not a typo.</p>\n<p><b>Weave Magic (Ch.14)</b> has its own dedicated reference with the full Shaping, Spell Effect, and Weave Reaction tables &mdash; open <b>TBE: Weave Magic Reference</b> elsewhere in this journal compendium. This page covers the core resolution mechanic (Ch.2), Death Threshold/Lethality Level (Ch.7), and Combat &amp; Wounds (Ch.9, Ch.11).</p>\n<h3>Chapter 2: Core Mechanics</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\"><tr><th>p.</th><th>What the macro computes</th><th>Used by</th><th>The book, verbatim</th></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>A skill roll is percentile dice (tens die + ones die) read as a number 1-100, compared to the skill value. Equal-to-or-under succeeds.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll, TBE: Opposed Roll, TBE: Attack (shared roll code in _lib.js)</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;If the roll is over the skill value, it fails. If the number rolled is equal to or less than the skill value, it succeeds.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>16</td><td>A skill&#x27;s Score is the tens digit of its value, used only where a rule says so, and never changed by modifiers.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll (skills over 100), TBE: Character Wizard</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;A skill also has a Score (the tens digit of a skill value). The Score is used only in specific cases, indicated when relevant, and is never altered by modifiers.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>On a success, the tens die shown is the number of Success Levels (SL). A 0 on the tens die counts as 1 SL, not 10 -- this is a different rule from Weave Mastery&#x27;s 0-counts-as-10.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll, TBE: Opposed Roll, TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;When you roll your skill value or under, you succeed, and the number showing on the tens die is the number of Success Levels (SLs). The Success Level represents the quality of success and, when relevant to the outcome, can have various effects based on the particular skill. On any successful skill roll, a 0 on the tens die counts as 1 SL.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>Doubles under your skill, or rolling the skill value exactly, is a critical success: +3 SL.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll, TBE: Opposed Roll, TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;A critical success is an extraordinary skill roll result, and is achieved when you roll doubles under your skill, or when you roll your skill value exactly. A critical success adds +3 Success Levels to your roll.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>Doubles over your skill value is a critical failure.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll, TBE: Opposed Roll, TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;A critical failure, on the other hand, can carry extra consequences. Rolling doubles over your skill value is a critical failure.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>01-05 always succeeds (even at skill 0 or below, which still succeeds on 01-04 and crits on 05); 99-00 always fails.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll, TBE: Opposed Roll, TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;A roll of 01-05 is always a success, regardless of skill value. Consequently, if your skill value is ever modified to zero or below, it still succeeds on a roll of 01-04, and critically succeeds on a 05. A roll of 99-00 is always a failure (and if over your skill, a critical failure), regardless of skill value.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>At skill 99 or 100, rolling the exact value is a failure, not a critical success.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll, TBE: Opposed Roll, TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;In the rare case you have a skill value of 99 or 100, rolling your skill value exactly is still a failure, not a critical success.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>A modified skill of 100+ can never critically fail; on a success it adds bonus SL equal to the tens digit of (skill - 100), minimum 1.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll, TBE: Opposed Roll, TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;These skills still fail on a roll of 99-00, but can no longer critically fail. If a skill ever has (or is modified to) a value over 100, roll it normally, but a success will benefit from extra SLs. To determine how many, subtract 100 from the skill value. The skill&#x27;s resulting tens die value (the Score) acts as extra SLs (with a minimum of 1) on a successful skill roll.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>19</td><td>A standard (unopposed) roll needs only 1 SL to succeed; extra SLs only matter when the specific skill or situation says so.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Skill Roll</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;A standard roll requires only 1 SL to succeed. However, additional Success Levels can sometimes produce a better outcome.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>19</td><td>Opposed rolls: whoever earns more SL wins (0 SL can still win). Tie-breaks in order: higher die roll, then higher modified skill, then critical beats non-critical; a normal failure beats a critical failure.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Opposed Roll</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;Both sides roll their skills and whoever earns more SLs is the winner. It&#x27;s possible to win an opposed roll with 0 SLs&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n</table>\n<h3>Chapter 7: Character Creation</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\"><tr><th>p.</th><th>What the macro computes</th><th>Used by</th><th>The book, verbatim</th></tr>\n<tr><td>86</td><td>Death Threshold starts at 20 and rises 2 per Attribute point spent on it -- the ceiling on total lethal Wound Points before death.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Character Wizard, the character sheet</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;Death Threshold (DT) is the maximum number of total lethal Wound Points you can suffer in all combined hit locations before you are killed. A Starting Death Threshold is 20. A Increase the Death Threshold by 2 per Attribute point spent.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>87</td><td>Lethality Level = 1/3 of Death Threshold, rounded up. In Shock with total lethal WP over LL means Dying.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Character Wizard, TBE: Attack (Dying check)</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;Lethality Level (LL) determines the point at which you start dying when you&#x27;ve fallen in Shock. A A character&#x27;s Lethality Level is equal to 1/3 of their Death Threshold, rounded up. A If they drop in Shock and their total lethal Wound Points exceed their Lethality Level, they are now Dying.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n</table>\n<h3>Chapter 9: Equipment</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\"><tr><th>p.</th><th>What the macro computes</th><th>Used by</th><th>The book, verbatim</th></tr>\n<tr><td>132</td><td>A weapon&#x27;s Piercing value bypasses that many Armor Points from worn armor. It does not reduce a shield&#x27;s AP.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;Piercing.............Weapon bypasses this number of Armor Points from worn Armor. It does not reduce a shield&#x27;s AP&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>132</td><td>Wound points marked NL (non-lethal) do not count against Death Threshold or Lethality Level.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack, TBE: Wounds &amp; Recovery</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;NL.....................Non-lethal. Wound points marked NL do not count against the Death Threshold or Lethality Level&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n</table>\n<h3>Chapter 11: Wounds, Healing &amp; Perils</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\"><tr><th>p.</th><th>What the macro computes</th><th>Used by</th><th>The book, verbatim</th></tr>\n<tr><td>163</td><td>Pierce Armor maneuver (4 SLs, target in Reinforced Leather or better): the attack gains Piercing 3 on the struck location, with no effect if that location has 3 AP or less.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;Pierce Armor (PA) Requirements: 4 SLs, opponent in Reinforced Leather armor or better Exploit a flaw or weak point in your opponent&#x27;s rigid armor. The attack gains Piercing 3 on the struck location. This has no effect if that location has 3 AP or less (Leather, Quilt, or Padding).&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>171</td><td>Wound = attacker&#x27;s DoS + weapon base damage, minus the struck location&#x27;s Armor Points (plus any Shield AP), floored appropriately by the macro.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;A successful attack adds the attacker&#x27;s DoS to the weapon&#x27;s base damage. Unless the attacker performed the Choose Location maneuver, the attacker&#x27;s ones die determines the General Hit Location of the strike&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>171</td><td>Subtract the location&#x27;s Armor + any Shield AP from incoming damage; the remainder is the wound&#x27;s WP.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;Subtract the location&#x27;s Armor + any Shield AP from the incoming damage. The remaining amount is the WP of the wound.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>172</td><td>Every new wound rolls Wound Die (d10, red) + Toughness against the location&#x27;s new total WP. Equal-or-under = Impaired. A natural 10 (&quot;0&quot;) always avoids impairment regardless of total.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;When suffering a wound, immediately roll the Wound Die (a red d10) and add the character&#x27;s Toughness. If the result is equal to or less than the new total Wound Points in the wounded location (from all wounds there), mark the location as Impaired. If the roll is a natural 10 (&quot;0&quot;) on the Wound Die, the location automatically avoids impairment, regardless of the location&#x27;s Wound Point total.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>172</td><td>A second Impairment on any location drops you in Shock (minutes = the failed Wound Die face); a second Body Impairment drops you in Shock immediately, no Endurance roll. 3 Resolve avoids dropping in Shock.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;If any of a character&#x27;s hit locations are Impaired a second time, they immediately drop in Shock (and also unconscious if from a Head impairment) for a number of minutes equal to the number showing on the failed Wound Die. A second Body impairment causes the character to immediately drop in Shock; there is no Endurance roll to avoid it. Once no longer in Shock, the character can act normally. A PC can spend 3 Resolve to avoid dropping in Shock.&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>172</td><td>First-time Impairment per location: Body needs an Endurance roll or Shock; Arm/Leg/Head effects split on whether the failed Wound Die came up odd or even.</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\">TBE: Attack</td><td style=\"font-size:90%\"><i>&ldquo;The first time a location is Impaired, the character suffers the following effects&rdquo;</i></td></tr>\n</table>\n<h3>What this page does not cover yet</h3><p>Character-creation point costs (races, careers, Talent prerequisites) and Equipment silver-piece costs are hand-transcribed with a lighter verification pass than the rules above, and aren't included here yet so this page never claims more rigor than actually exists behind it &mdash; see BACKLOG.md. Weave Magic (Ch.14) is fully covered, just in its own reference page rather than duplicated here.</p>\n"}];

(async () => {
  if (!game.user.isGM) return ui.notifications.error("Run the TBE installer as GM.");
  const report = { macros: 0, tables: 0, journal: 0, errors: [] };
  const fail = (what, err) => {
    report.errors.push(what + ": " + (err?.message ?? err));
    console.error("TBE | " + what, err);
  };

  console.log("TBE | installing on Foundry " + game.version + " | system " + game.system.id + " " + game.system.version);

  /* 1. Folders. Optional, so a folder failure never blocks the install. */
  const getFolder = async (name, type) => {
    try {
      const found = game.folders.find((f) => f.name === name && f.type === type);
      return found ?? (await Folder.create({ name, type }));
    } catch (err) {
      fail("folder " + type, err);
      return null;
    }
  };
  const macroFolder = await getFolder("TBE Solo", "Macro");
  const tableFolder = await getFolder("TBE Solo", "RollTable");
  const journalFolder = await getFolder("TBE Solo", "JournalEntry");

  /* 2. Macros first. Existing ones are updated in place so hotbar slots keep working. */
  for (const m of TBE_MACROS) {
    try {
      const old = game.macros.getName(m.name);
      if (old) {
        await old.update({ command: m.command, type: "script", scope: "global" });
        report.macros++;
        continue;
      }
      const base = { name: m.name, type: "script", scope: "global", command: m.command };
      if (macroFolder) base.folder = macroFolder.id;
      try {
        await Macro.create(Object.assign({ img: m.img }, base));
      } catch (imgErr) {
        console.warn("TBE | macro image rejected, retrying without it", imgErr);
        await Macro.create(base);
      }
      report.macros++;
    } catch (err) {
      fail("macro " + m.name, err);
    }
  }

  /* 3. Tables. Created empty, then filled with whichever result shape this build accepts. */
  const short = (s) => (s.length > 48 ? s.slice(0, 48) + "..." : s);
  const shapes = [
    (r) => ({ range: r[0], weight: 1, type: "text", name: short(r[1]), description: r[1] }),
    (r) => ({ range: r[0], weight: 1, name: short(r[1]), description: r[1] }),
    (r) => ({ range: r[0], weight: 1, type: 0, text: r[1] }),
    (r) => ({ range: r[0], weight: 1, text: r[1] })
  ];

  for (const t of TBE_TABLES) {
    try {
      const old = game.tables.getName(t.name);
      /* Never clobber an Empires List you have filled in. */
      if (old && t.name === "TBE: Empires List") { report.tables++; continue; }
      if (old) await old.delete();
      const base = { name: t.name, formula: "1d100", replacement: true, displayRoll: true };
      if (tableFolder) base.folder = tableFolder.id;
      const table = await RollTable.create(base);
      let filled = false;
      for (const shape of shapes) {
        try {
          const made = await table.createEmbeddedDocuments("TableResult", t.rows.map(shape));
          if (made && made.length) { filled = true; break; }
        } catch (shapeErr) {
          console.warn("TBE | result shape rejected for " + t.name, shapeErr);
        }
      }
      if (!filled) fail("table results " + t.name, "no accepted result shape");
      else report.tables++;
    } catch (err) {
      fail("table " + t.name, err);
    }
  }

  /* 4. Journals. */
  for (const j of TBE_JOURNALS) {
    try {
      const oldJournal = game.journal.getName(j.name);
      if (oldJournal) await oldJournal.delete();
      const base = {
        name: j.name,
        pages: [{ name: j.page, type: "text", text: { content: j.content, format: 1 } }]
      };
      if (journalFolder) base.folder = journalFolder.id;
      await JournalEntry.create(base);
      report.journal++;
    } catch (err) {
      fail("journal " + j.name, err);
    }
  }

  const summary =
    "TBE Solo: " + report.macros + "/" + TBE_MACROS.length + " macros, " +
    report.tables + "/" + TBE_TABLES.length + " tables, " + report.journal + "/" + TBE_JOURNALS.length + " journals.";
  console.log("TBE | " + summary, report);
  if (report.errors.length) {
    ui.notifications.error(summary + " " + report.errors.length + " problem(s), see console (F12).");
    console.error("TBE | problems:", report.errors);
  } else {
    ui.notifications.info(summary + " Done.");
  }
})();
